1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,982
Cent #8820.

2
00:00:04,421 --> 00:00:06,573
A penny struck in 1972

3
00:00:06,613 --> 00:00:07,698
with no mint mark,

4
00:00:07,887 --> 00:00:10,195
generally indicating the Philadelphia mint.

5
00:00:11,035 --> 00:00:12,608
Two patinas share its face.

6
00:00:13,647 --> 00:00:15,507
Significant Red: in the Traits

7
00:00:15,548 --> 00:00:18,044
Atlas, red that is "a major visual actor",

8
00:00:18,234 --> 00:00:19,777
strong across several zones.

9
00:00:20,617 --> 00:00:23,074
183 Cents carry it.

10
00:00:24,713 --> 00:00:25,908
And Notable Verdigris:

11
00:00:26,097 --> 00:00:28,737
"localised but clearly visible patches or films".

12
00:00:29,178 --> 00:00:32,121
On Cent #8820 the green gathers

13
00:00:32,161 --> 00:00:34,250
in the lettering and along the rim,

14
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:36,683
with grey-green patches on Lincoln's face

15
00:00:36,723 --> 00:00:38,719
like daubs of paint laid by time.

16
00:00:39,759 --> 00:00:42,049
29 Cents carry exactly this pair,

17
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:43,204
and nothing else.

18
00:00:44,643 --> 00:00:46,307
Both are oxidation of copper.

19
00:00:46,747 --> 00:00:49,658
In the Atlas, red "marks a transitional oxidation

20
00:00:49,698 --> 00:00:52,338
phase between bright copper and darker patinas".

21
00:00:53,578 --> 00:00:55,747
Beside Cent #8653,

22
00:00:55,938 --> 00:00:58,634
struck the same year and close to mint state,

23
00:00:58,823 --> 00:01:01,795
you can see how far Cent #8820

24
00:01:01,835 --> 00:01:03,500
had moved from bright copper.

25
00:01:03,939 --> 00:01:07,055
As money, the two were worth the same: one cent.

26
00:01:08,296 --> 00:01:09,444
Permanence and decay.

27
00:01:10,683 --> 00:01:13,566
Walter Benjamin, in his 1936 essay

28
00:01:13,605 --> 00:01:14,597
"The Work of Art

29
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:16,790
in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction",

30
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:19,445
wrote that the history of a work of art

31
00:01:19,486 --> 00:01:21,453
includes "the changes which it may

32
00:01:21,493 --> 00:01:24,391
have suffered in physical condition over the years

33
00:01:24,432 --> 00:01:27,266
as well as the various changes in its ownership."

34
00:01:28,305 --> 00:01:30,245
A penny is mechanically reproduced

35
00:01:30,286 --> 00:01:32,266
from the start, one of billions.

36
00:01:32,706 --> 00:01:36,590
Yet time gave Cent #8820 a history of its own.

37
00:01:37,629 --> 00:01:39,036
But Benjamin also wrote:

38
00:01:39,225 --> 00:01:40,890
"The presence of the original

39
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,828
is the prerequisite to the concept of authenticity."

40
00:01:44,668 --> 00:01:46,147
The penny itself is gone.

41
00:01:47,987 --> 00:01:49,274
Through Benjamin's lens,

42
00:01:49,463 --> 00:01:51,781
CENTS keeps both halves of that history:

43
00:01:51,971 --> 00:01:53,819
the photograph keeps the changes

44
00:01:53,859 --> 00:01:55,054
in physical condition,

45
00:01:55,243 --> 00:01:57,579
and Bitcoin's ledger records each transfer

46
00:01:57,620 --> 00:01:59,846
of the satoshi that carries its image.

47
00:02:01,085 --> 00:02:03,737
Cent #8820, a great Cent.
